# The compiler's types, each kind a struct (#593). Inference reads a type
# off every expression, and pinning, lowering, and the emitter work with
# what it reads. A type was a tagged array, `["maybe", ["integer"]]`: a
# String beside an array, nested to any depth, which a native array cannot
# be. Each kind moves here as it becomes a struct, and is written by its
# fields' names.
#
# A file of its own, required ahead of everything that names a type, since
# a file runs its constants before it declares its structs, and the
# compiler's constants are full of types.

# An array, by the type of what it holds: the element's type, or unknown
# where nothing has said.
struct ArrayType
  element
end

# A type's name as a value (ADR 0074): what `Name.new` and a type function
# are called on, and what `value.class` answers, by the name.
struct ClassType
  name
end

# A value of one of the program's enums, by the enum's name.
struct EnumType
  name
end

# A hash, by the types of its keys and of their values.
struct HashType
  key
  value
end

# What a call hands a def by name (#536): the label, and the type of what
# is handed. A call's arguments are its placed types and then these, and
# the combination of a def's types is named by them.
struct KeywordType
  label
  type
end

# A maybe of a type (ADR 0040 §3): the wrapper, never a union, holding the
# type of the value where it is there.
struct MaybeType
  inner
end

# A range (ADR 0081), by the type of its ends, or unknown where they are
# not known.
struct RangeType
  element
end

# An instance of one of the program's structs, by the struct's name, or
# of whichever of them it is, by `*` (#572).
struct StructType
  name
end
